{"step_index":1272,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:44:39Z","thinking":"**Investigating Roman Domination Bound**\n\nEvery connected graph seemingly satisfies the inequality. Need to determine the mathematical relationship between the radius and Roman domination. Consider a minimum Roman dominating function $D$ and its weight $\\gamma_R(G)$. Defining $V_2$, $V_1$ and $V_0$ based on function values will be useful.\n\n\n**Bounding Graph Radius via Domination**\n\nThe set $S = V_2 \\cup V_1$ forms a dominating set, and $|S| \\le \\gamma_R(G)$. A dominating set's size impacts the graph's radius. Considering a path graph $P_n$ provides an example. The number of vertices on $P_n$ is bounded as $n \\le 3 |V_2| + |V_1| \\le \\frac{3}{2} \\gamma_R$.\n\nThe path's radius,  $\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$, is bounded by $\\frac{3}{4} \\gamma_R$. This suggests a potential relationship between the radius and $\\gamma_R$. It appears that the inequality $\\text{radius} \\le \\gamma_R - 1$ doesn't necessarily hold. However, radius on a path can be smaller, for larger graphs.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python3 -c \"\nimport networkx as nx\n\nfor n in range(2, 20):\n    for p in [0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5]:\n        for _ in range(50):\n            G = nx.erdos_renyi_graph(n, p)\n            if nx.is_connected(G):\n                rad = nx.radius(G)\n                assert rad <= n // 2, f'Violation: n={n}, rad={rad}'\n\nprint('Radius <= n // 2 always holds for connected graphs!')\n\"","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","RunPersistent":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Running command","toolSummary":"Check radius <= n // 2"}}]}
