{"step_index":1274,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:44:46Z","thinking":"**Investigating graph radius theorem**\n\nConsidering if the radius of a graph $G$ is less than or equal to $\\lfloor n / 2 \\rfloor$ where $n$ is the number of vertices. The diameter $D$ is at most $n-1$. Observing the relationship between radius and diameter, particularly in a cycle graph $C_{2k+1}$, confirms that the radius is equal to $\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$.\n\n\n**Examining roman domination and radius**\n\nTrying to relate small roman domination to graph radius.  A small roman weight with a large number of vertices suggests high-degree vertices labeled with 2. Such a vertex can dominate many neighbors. These neighbors are all at distance 1.\n\nIf two vertices have labels of 2, the neighbors of each are within distance 1 of them. Undominated vertices must be labeled 1. Thus, every vertex is within distance 1 of a vertex with label 2 or has a label of 1.\n\n\n**Analyzing vertex domination in graphs**\n\nEvery vertex $v$ with a label of 0 is dominated by a vertex with label 2. Vertices labeled 2 dominate themselves and their neighbors.  The set of vertices not in the union of the closed neighborhoods of vertices labeled 2 is a subset of the vertices labeled 1.\n\nIf there are two vertices with label 2,  the number of vertices labeled 1 can be calculated as  romanWeight - 4. Considering a path between the two vertices labeled 2 could provide additional insights. Further consideration is whether a simple observation can be applied.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python3 -c \"\nimport networkx as nx\n\nmax_rad = {n: 0 for n in range(2, 10)}\nfor n in range(2, 9):\n    # check all trees on n vertices (trees maximize radius)\n    import itertools\n    # trees on n vertices:\n    # Actually, maximum radius on n vertices is achieved by P_n!\n    P = nx.path